@node Browsing local files
@section Browsing local files

@findex eww-extras-browse-file
The command @code{eww-extras-browse-file} opens a local file in EWW@.  It
tries several sources for the file path: an explicit FILE argument@comma{} the
filename at point@comma{} the current buffer's file@comma{} or a prompt.  This is
useful for previewing local HTML files or documents in the EWW renderer.

@node Generating files from URLs
@section Generating files from URLs

@findex eww-extras-url-to-pdf
@findex eww-extras-url-to-html
The command @code{eww-extras-url-to-pdf} generates a PDF file from a URL
using headless Chrome's print-to-PDF feature.  The URL is determined
from context (the current EWW page@comma{} kill ring@comma{} or a prompt).  The output
file is saved to the downloads directory (@code{paths-dir-downloads}) with a
name derived from the Bib@TeX{} key (if called from Ebib or Bib@TeX{} mode)
or the buffer name.

The command @code{eww-extras-url-to-html} generates an HTML file from a URL
using headless Chrome's DOM dump feature.  When called from a
bibliographic context@comma{} the file name is based on the Bib@TeX{} key;
otherwise@comma{} it uses a slugified version of the page title.

Both commands accept an optional CALLBACK function that is called with
the output file path and Bib@TeX{} key upon completion.  This enables
workflows like automatically attaching the generated file to a Bib@TeX{}
entry.

The commands run asynchronously in a fresh headless Chrome profile.  They do
not read a personal Chrome profile@comma{} retain cookies between runs@comma{} load browser
extensions@comma{} or require an exported cookie file.  The first run installs the
exact locked renderer dependencies in @samp{~/Library/Caches/eww-extras-renderer};
later runs reuse that cache.  The dependency tree and browser profile are
never created in the dotfiles repository or Google Drive.

Before saving the file@comma{} the renderer uses DuckDuckGo Autoconsent to opt out of
supported consent managers.  It then checks every frame for a remaining
blocking consent prompt and removes non-consent residual overlays@comma{} such as
subscribe or newsletter dialogs.  A dialog that merely cites a privacy policy
or notice is a residual overlay@comma{} not a consent prompt@comma{} and an in-flow element
is never removed on the strength of its class name alone.  If a consent prompt
remains@comma{} the process fails and does not create or modify the requested output.
The renderer is intended for public pages; it does not carry authentication
from a personal browser session.  Rendering has a 27-second internal deadline
and a 30-second hard process limit.

@node Adding readability exceptions
@section Adding readability exceptions

@findex eww-extras-add-domain-to-readable-exceptions
The command @code{eww-extras-add-domain-to-readable-exceptions} prompts for a
domain pattern and adds it to the readability exceptions file.  The
domain of the current EWW page (or a URL in the kill ring) is offered as
the default input.  After adding the exception@comma{} it reloads the
exceptions list and refreshes the current page.

This command provides a quick way to permanently opt out of readability
mode for sites where it strips important content.

@node Vimium-like navigation
@section Vimium-like navigation

@findex eww-extras-edit-current-url
@findex eww-extras-open-with-recent-kill-ring
@findex eww-extras-go-up-url-hierarchy
@findex eww-extras-go-to-root-url-hierarchy
The command @code{eww-extras-edit-current-url} pre-fills the minibuffer with
the current EWW URL and lets you edit it or enter a new search query.
With a prefix argument@comma{} the result opens in a new EWW buffer.

The command @code{eww-extras-open-with-recent-kill-ring} opens the most
recent kill ring entry as a URL in EWW@.  With a prefix argument@comma{} it
opens in a new EWW buffer.

The command @code{eww-extras-go-up-url-hierarchy} navigates one level up in
the URL path.  For example@comma{} from @samp{example.com/a/b/c} it navigates to
@samp{example.com/a/b}.

The command @code{eww-extras-go-to-root-url-hierarchy} navigates to the root
domain of the current URL@comma{} stripping all path components.

@node Switching renderers
@section Switching renderers

@findex eww-extras-open-with-xwidget
@findex eww-extras-open-with-eww
The command @code{eww-extras-open-with-xwidget} opens the current EWW page
in xwidget-webkit@comma{} providing a full WebKit rendering.  This is useful
when EWW's text-mode rendering is insufficient for a page's layout.
The command must be called from an EWW buffer.

The command @code{eww-extras-open-with-eww} performs the reverse operation:
it takes the URL from the current xwidget-webkit session and opens it
in EWW@.  This is useful when you want EWW's readability mode or
text-based navigation for a page you are viewing in xwidget-webkit.

@node Bibliography integration
@section Bibliography integration

@findex eww-extras-add-entry
The command @code{eww-extras-add-entry} adds the current EWW page's URL to a
bibliography file using @code{zotra-extras-add-entry}.  This command is
available only in @code{eww-mode} buffers.

