@node Readability management
@section Readability management

@findex eww-extras-readable-autoview
@findex eww-extras-set-readable-exceptions-from-file
The function @code{eww-extras-readable-autoview} is added to
@code{eww-after-render-hook}.  It automatically calls @code{eww-readable} unless
the current URL matches a pattern in @code{eww-extras-readable-exceptions} or
the page has no source content to process.  If the readability heuristic
selects a non-visible node (producing an empty buffer) or a node
covering only a tiny fraction of the article content@comma{} the function falls
back to displaying the full page.  This guards against cases where
navigation-heavy elements drag down the full content node's score@comma{}
causing a narrow subsection to be selected instead.

The function @code{eww-extras-set-readable-exceptions-from-file} reads the
exception patterns from @code{eww-extras-readable-exceptions-file} and
populates the @code{eww-extras-readable-exceptions} variable.  It is called
automatically at load time and after adding a new exception.

@node YouTube handling
@section YouTube handling

@findex eww-extras-browse-youtube
The function @code{eww-extras-browse-youtube} is designed as a handler for
YouTube URLs (e.g.@comma{} in @code{browse-url-handlers}).  When called with a
YouTube URL@comma{} it kills the current EWW buffer@comma{} ensures the external
video player is running (defaulting to @samp{mpv})@comma{} and fetches the video
transcript using @code{elfeed-tube-fetch}.  This provides a workflow where
you read the transcript in Emacs while watching the video in an external
player.

@node File generation internals
@section File generation internals

@findex eww-extras-url-to-file
An explicit KEY determines the output stem independently of the selected buffer. Optional OUTPUT-PATH permits a unique staging file for each operation@comma{} including simultaneous requests for the same key in different databases. Optional ERROR-CALLBACK receives terminal failures; without it@comma{} the sentinel retains its existing error behavior. Stop and continuation events do not complete the conversion@comma{} and each terminal callback runs once.

@findex eww-extras-url-to-file-make-command
@findex eww-extras-url-to-file-sentinel
@findex eww-extras-run-callback
The function @code{eww-extras-url-to-file} is the core logic shared by
@code{eww-extras-url-to-pdf} and @code{eww-extras-url-to-html}.  It determines
the URL and output file name from context@comma{} constructs and starts the
headless Chrome process@comma{} and sets up the process sentinel.  An optional
@code{key} argument lets callers supply the Bib@TeX{} key directly@comma{} bypassing
the buffer-derived lookup.

The function @code{eww-extras-url-to-file-make-command} builds a direct argument
list for the renderer wrapper.  The wrapper verifies the lock-hash dependency
cache@comma{} installs it with @samp{npm ci --ignore-scripts} when necessary@comma{} and launches
the configured system Chrome through @samp{playwright-core}.  It does not use a
shell command@comma{} @samp{npx}@comma{} or a Playwright-managed browser download.

The function @code{eww-extras-url-to-file-sentinel} creates a process
sentinel that requires both a zero exit status and a nonempty output before it
runs the callback.  Each render has a private diagnostics buffer@comma{} so concurrent
PDF and HTML jobs cannot mix their errors. Standard error joins the main
process output@comma{} preserving the renderer's diagnostic without an extra pipe
sentinel's completion message. Failed output is never attached.

The renderer rejects unsuccessful HTTP responses for the main document@comma{}
including redirect targets and later document navigations. Failed images or
embedded frames do not turn a successful article response into an error. Known
browser challenge pages@comma{} including Cloudflare's block-page structure@comma{} are also
rejected even when the server reports HTTP success. These failures preserve an
existing destination and never install a newly rendered error page. Previously
verified local HTML can be supplied explicitly with a @samp{file:} URL@.

Residual UI cleanup requires dialog semantics or an out-of-flow element with an
overlay identity; a large fixed or sticky reading pane is not itself an overlay.
An unnamed dialog backdrop is removed only when it contains no other content.
Cleanup refuses to remove a main/article element or a first-level heading
outside a dialog. Such ambiguous matches fail the render and preserve an
existing destination@comma{} rather than attaching a partial document.

The function @code{eww-extras-run-callback} safely executes the optional
callback function with the output file and Bib@TeX{} key as arguments.

@node Patches
@chapter Patches

@menu
* Readability node selection::
@end menu

@node Readability node selection
@section Readability node selection

@findex eww-highest-readability
The built-in function @code{eww-highest-readability} selects the DOM node
with the highest readability score for @code{eww-readable}.  It replaces the
current best node with a deeper child whenever the child scores higher
and contains more than 100 words.  On pages where navigation-heavy
elements (navboxes@comma{} table of contents@comma{} categories) drag down the full
content node's aggregate score@comma{} a small subsection—such as the lead
paragraph—can win the readability contest over the full article.

This package uses @code{el-patch} to raise the minimum word threshold from a
fixed 100 to @code{max(100@comma{} 1/3 of the current best node's word count)}.
This ensures a child must contain a substantial fraction of the
parent's text to replace it@comma{} preventing narrow subsections from winning
over broader content areas.

